1
00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:04.104
In this lesson, you'll
build a simple social media management

2
00:00:04.104 --> 00:00:07.107
agent using OpenAI's
Structured Output API.

3
00:00:07.374 --> 00:00:10.810
You'll also learn the basics of specifying
your desired output structure

4
00:00:10.810 --> 00:00:12.145
using Pydantic.

5
00:00:12.145 --> 00:00:14.781
All right. Let's delve in.

6
00:00:14.781 --> 00:00:15.715
Structured output

7
00:00:15.715 --> 00:00:19.652
often uses what is called a JSON schema
to enforce output structure.

8
00:00:19.719 --> 00:00:23.857
JSON schemas or standards
that describe the shape of a JSON message.

9
00:00:23.990 --> 00:00:25.492
So these schemas provide

10
00:00:25.492 --> 00:00:28.495
the type information
that is required for structured output.

11
00:00:28.561 --> 00:00:29.596
As an example,

12
00:00:29.596 --> 00:00:33.433
here's a JSON message with Dolly Parton's
name, age, and email address.

13
00:00:33.633 --> 00:00:37.637
So we can see here we have keys
on the left such as name, age and email

14
00:00:37.804 --> 00:00:40.707
followed by their values
on the right, Dolly Parton,

15
00:00:40.707 --> 00:00:43.710
aged 79, and dolly@test.com.

16
00:00:44.244 --> 00:00:45.245
Let's take a look

17
00:00:45.245 --> 00:00:49.416
at an example of a Json schema
that describes a person like Dolly Parton.

18
00:00:49.616 --> 00:00:53.253
Here we can see this is also Json
and includes all of our keys

19
00:00:53.253 --> 00:00:56.790
such as name is an email
as well as the types of those keys.

20
00:00:56.890 --> 00:01:00.660
Such as title equals
name and type equals string.

21
00:01:01.127 --> 00:01:02.896
Let's talk a little bit about pydantic.

22
00:01:02.896 --> 00:01:07.000
Maintaining JSON schemas
by hand is difficult and prone to error.

23
00:01:07.067 --> 00:01:09.202
As you saw, they're pretty complicated

24
00:01:09.202 --> 00:01:12.839
and for anything that's non-trivial
they can be an enormous pain to work with.

25
00:01:12.939 --> 00:01:17.939
So, AI engineers often use pydantic
to describe model output structure.

26
00:01:18.178 --> 00:01:21.481
Pydantic is an open source
data validation library

27
00:01:21.581 --> 00:01:25.452
where you can flexibly define data
structures for use in your application.

28
00:01:25.552 --> 00:01:26.853
It's also feature-rich.

29
00:01:26.853 --> 00:01:30.290
You should go check out all the things
that it supports in the documentation.

30
00:01:30.623 --> 00:01:33.626
We won't be able to cover
all of the tools that it has available.

31
00:01:33.626 --> 00:01:37.464
Using pydantic makes language
model outputs programmable.

32
00:01:37.697 --> 00:01:39.732
As we'll see in just a second.

33
00:01:39.732 --> 00:01:44.337
So here is how you would write
a user class inside of pydantic.

34
00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:49.370
Inheriting from base model is all
you need to make a user a pydantic class.

35
00:01:49.509 --> 00:01:53.446
Next, you're going to use type annotations
to define the structure that your model

36
00:01:53.446 --> 00:01:54.147
should generate.

37
00:01:54.147 --> 00:01:57.283
For example,
name can be any string like Dolly Parton.

38
00:01:57.450 --> 00:02:02.450
Age can be any integer like 79, and email
can be either nothing or a string.

39
00:02:02.622 --> 00:02:04.124
Note, however, that OpenAI

40
00:02:04.124 --> 00:02:07.794
does not allow you
to enforce the formatting of email or i.e.

41
00:02:07.861 --> 00:02:09.829
always in the form of test,

42
00:02:09.829 --> 00:02:13.199
at test.com . Outlines and Instructor,
and a few other tools do.

43
00:02:13.199 --> 00:02:17.303
So let's build a social media customer
support agent.

44
00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:18.738
We have a lot of users

45
00:02:18.738 --> 00:02:22.575
who like to tweet at us
saying at Tech Corp your app is amazing.

46
00:02:22.575 --> 00:02:24.244
The new design is perfect.

47
00:02:24.244 --> 00:02:28.081
And then we're going to put this inside
of our customer support agent, and

48
00:02:28.081 --> 00:02:32.152
then we're going to output some structured
data telling us what product it's about,

49
00:02:32.285 --> 00:02:35.388
what the sentiment is from the user,
whether or not we should respond,

50
00:02:35.588 --> 00:02:38.725
and a support ticket
if anything happens to be broken.

51
00:02:38.925 --> 00:02:41.327
All right. So, let's go to the code.

52
00:02:41.327 --> 00:02:43.563
We're going to start
with a little bit of housekeeping here.

53
00:02:43.563 --> 00:02:47.901
So these two lines just filter out
some warnings that we may not want to see

54
00:02:47.901 --> 00:02:48.735
while we're learning.

55
00:02:48.735 --> 00:02:53.239
And here's how you load the OpenAI API key
that we're going to use.

56
00:02:53.373 --> 00:02:56.543
These three lines
go and set up your OpenAI

57
00:02:56.543 --> 00:02:59.546
API key that DeepLearning.AI
has configured.

58
00:02:59.612 --> 00:03:04.612
Here's how you initialize
the OpenAI client using the OpenAI key.

59
00:03:04.784 --> 00:03:07.921
So let's see an example
of how we define structure with pydantic.

60
00:03:08.254 --> 00:03:12.625
We'll start by pasting in this user class
that we added from the slides.

61
00:03:12.725 --> 00:03:16.296
So let's generate a user object
using OpenAI.

62
00:03:16.396 --> 00:03:19.499
Here, we're going to use the client
that we defined.

63
00:03:19.499 --> 00:03:23.603
And then we're going to call dot Beta dot
chat dot completions dot parse.

64
00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:25.071
We're going to select our model, here

65
00:03:25.071 --> 00:03:26.839
we're going to use GPT-4o-mini.

66
00:03:26.839 --> 00:03:29.509
Then we're going to provide
some messages. Here,

67
00:03:29.509 --> 00:03:33.379
the first message is the system prompt
which says you're a helpful assistant.

68
00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:36.983
And then we're going to pass the user
prompt that you might type into ChatGPT.

69
00:03:37.283 --> 00:03:39.419
And then we're just going to say
make up a user.

70
00:03:39.419 --> 00:03:43.189
Then in response format
we specify the user class we just made.

71
00:03:43.256 --> 00:03:45.191
When we run this, it'll take a second.

72
00:03:45.191 --> 00:03:48.194
And then OpenAI will provide us an user.

73
00:03:48.428 --> 00:03:52.432
Let's look at this user
by taking it out of our completion object.

74
00:03:52.832 --> 00:03:57.503
Normally, OpenAI returns a list of choices
when you request multiple values.

75
00:03:57.503 --> 00:04:00.039
But here, we've only requested
one completion.

76
00:04:00.039 --> 00:04:01.474
Then, we're going to extract the message.

77
00:04:01.474 --> 00:04:04.711
And then the parse object inside of dot parsed.

78
00:04:04.777 --> 00:04:05.411
Let me run this.

79
00:04:05.411 --> 00:04:08.648
We see
we got a user named John Doe, age 30,

80
00:04:08.815 --> 00:04:11.818
with an email John Doe at example.com.

81
00:04:12.252 --> 00:04:15.255
Let's build
our social media management agent.

82
00:04:15.321 --> 00:04:19.459
Anytime you're working with structured
output, you typically define the structure

83
00:04:19.459 --> 00:04:22.862
that you want to see before you start
working with the rest of your code.

84
00:04:23.029 --> 00:04:26.733
In this case, we've added a mention
which is a pydantic class

85
00:04:26.899 --> 00:04:28.868
that has product and sentiment.

86
00:04:28.868 --> 00:04:31.504
And you'll see here
that these are literals, which means that

87
00:04:31.504 --> 00:04:35.074
the language model has to choose
between app, website or not applicable.

88
00:04:35.275 --> 00:04:38.278
Same thing with sentiment
has to be positive, negative or neutral.

89
00:04:38.378 --> 00:04:39.479
Next, the model can

90
00:04:39.479 --> 00:04:43.249
choose to respond to the user
so it is allowed to choose true or false.

91
00:04:43.316 --> 00:04:45.418
whether or not
the company needs to respond.

92
00:04:45.418 --> 00:04:48.521
And then it has a response
which is an optional string.

93
00:04:48.554 --> 00:04:51.224
So that can be either a string or nothing.

94
00:04:51.224 --> 00:04:54.527
This is the response
that we would provide to the user.

95
00:04:54.594 --> 00:04:58.264
Lastly, if the model determines
that a support ticket needs to be open,

96
00:04:58.264 --> 00:05:00.566
the model can write a description
for the developers.

97
00:05:00.566 --> 00:05:03.936
And this may also be either
nothing or string.

98
00:05:04.137 --> 00:05:06.906
Now, let's give ourselves
some example data.

99
00:05:06.906 --> 00:05:11.010
I'm going to call this the mentions list
and it includes a few examples.

100
00:05:11.010 --> 00:05:14.247
So we have a positive review of our app.

101
00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:15.682
They like the design.

102
00:05:15.682 --> 00:05:19.018
Next, there's a user
letting us know that the website is down

103
00:05:19.018 --> 00:05:20.219
and that we need to fix it.

104
00:05:20.219 --> 00:05:24.324
And then lastly we have "Hey at Tech Corp
you're so evil", which we probably don't

105
00:05:24.324 --> 00:05:25.825
want to respond to though.

106
00:05:25.825 --> 00:05:29.395
We might wish to generally address
concerns about our evilness.

107
00:05:29.629 --> 00:05:33.533
So here's how you add an analyze
mentioned function.

108
00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:38.104
This is how we take the user messages
that they sent to us,

109
00:05:38.471 --> 00:05:42.008
and then we construct this mention object
that we just designed.

110
00:05:42.308 --> 00:05:45.244
So here we're going to define a function
called analyze mention.

111
00:05:45.244 --> 00:05:48.114
We're going to provide the post
that the user sent to us.

112
00:05:48.114 --> 00:05:50.983
And then an optional keyword argument
for the personality,

113
00:05:50.983 --> 00:05:52.819
which we'll talk about in just a second.

114
00:05:52.819 --> 00:05:54.354
By default, this is friendly.

115
00:05:54.354 --> 00:05:57.323
As before we're going to use dot
beta, dot chat, dot completions,

116
00:05:57.323 --> 00:06:00.827
dot parse. Select our model
and provide a few messages.

117
00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:01.561
In this case,

118
00:06:01.561 --> 00:06:05.465
we've provided the system prompt
which says what we expect from the model.

119
00:06:05.598 --> 00:06:07.467
We want the model to provide the product

120
00:06:07.467 --> 00:06:09.469
mention, the mentioned sentiment,

121
00:06:09.469 --> 00:06:11.738
whether or not to respond.

122
00:06:11.738 --> 00:06:12.839
We let the model know

123
00:06:12.839 --> 00:06:16.142
that it should not respond
to inform inflammatory messages or bait,

124
00:06:16.309 --> 00:06:19.612
and we ask it for a customized message
to send to the user.

125
00:06:19.912 --> 00:06:23.082
Lastly, we ask the model
to provide an optional support ticket

126
00:06:23.282 --> 00:06:26.185
description
to upload to our ticket management system.

127
00:06:26.185 --> 00:06:29.455
Then, finally we let the model know
what its personality is.

128
00:06:29.622 --> 00:06:32.525
And then inside of the user prompt
we just add the mention.

129
00:06:32.525 --> 00:06:35.161
Lastly, we request a mention object.

130
00:06:35.161 --> 00:06:38.164
Then we're just going to return
the parsed object at the end.

131
00:06:38.731 --> 00:06:41.567
So let's go ahead and use this
analyze mention function.

132
00:06:41.567 --> 00:06:44.003
We're going to use the first mention
which if you remember

133
00:06:44.003 --> 00:06:45.972
was the positive review about our app.

134
00:06:45.972 --> 00:06:46.839
We're going to pass that

135
00:06:46.839 --> 00:06:50.743
to the are analyze mention function
and put it in processed mention.

136
00:06:50.843 --> 00:06:52.578
When we run this we'll see.

137
00:06:52.578 --> 00:06:54.480
Hey Techcorp, your app so amazing.

138
00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:57.617
And then we see the models
provided us with product app.

139
00:06:57.617 --> 00:07:00.653
So it's identifying that it's about
the app as well as positive sentiment.

140
00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:03.523
The model has flagged
that there needs to be a response.

141
00:07:03.523 --> 00:07:07.059
And then it includes a response that
we can choose to send back to the user.

142
00:07:07.360 --> 00:07:09.729
We're thrilled to hear that
you love the new design.

143
00:07:09.729 --> 00:07:11.664
And then lastly, there's no support ticket

144
00:07:11.664 --> 00:07:15.435
because there's no obvious issues
to address with our software.

145
00:07:15.601 --> 00:07:16.669
If you would like to adjust

146
00:07:16.669 --> 00:07:20.573
the personality of this model,
you can simply change the personality.

147
00:07:20.907 --> 00:07:23.242
I've changed the personality here
to be rude.

148
00:07:23.242 --> 00:07:25.645
In which case the model says
thanks for the praise.

149
00:07:25.645 --> 00:07:28.414
We're glad you love the new design,
but don't get too comfy.

150
00:07:28.414 --> 00:07:29.816
We're constantly improving,

151
00:07:29.816 --> 00:07:33.519
which could be rude or try playing around
with the different personalities.

152
00:07:33.619 --> 00:07:35.321
And for those who are curious,

153
00:07:35.321 --> 00:07:39.325
the underlying content that was generated
by the model is just Json.

154
00:07:39.559 --> 00:07:43.729
So when we print these out
using pedantic model dump JSON method,

155
00:07:44.063 --> 00:07:47.200
we can see what was generated
by the language model before

156
00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:50.169
it was passed into the pydantic object
that we have.

157
00:07:50.169 --> 00:07:52.538
In this case, we can see the raw text.

158
00:07:52.538 --> 00:07:55.041
Here's an example of something
you can play with on your own.

159
00:07:55.041 --> 00:07:56.042
In this example,

160
00:07:56.042 --> 00:08:00.246
you're going to create a user post
which has just a message inside of it.

161
00:08:00.446 --> 00:08:04.317
And then you're going to ask a language
model to act as if it was a person,

162
00:08:04.617 --> 00:08:07.620
and then provide tweets to techcorp.

163
00:08:07.653 --> 00:08:09.956
We describe roughly what's going on there.

164
00:08:09.956 --> 00:08:11.324
And then we can see the output.

165
00:08:11.324 --> 00:08:14.293
Hey, just wanted to shout out to TechCorp
for their amazing app.

166
00:08:14.293 --> 00:08:18.431
Now, we can provide that output
from the user

167
00:08:18.698 --> 00:08:21.701
and put it back into our analyze
mentioned function.

168
00:08:21.734 --> 00:08:24.804
When we see this, we're going to get app
positive sentiment.

169
00:08:24.904 --> 00:08:26.506
Thanks so much for your kind words.

170
00:08:26.506 --> 00:08:27.073
And of course

171
00:08:27.073 --> 00:08:30.710
the language model just seems to like
saying thanks so much for your kind words.

172
00:08:31.344 --> 00:08:35.882
So try adjusting this user post class.
In this example,

173
00:08:36.115 --> 00:08:40.486
I've added a user post with extras class
which is also a pydantic class

174
00:08:40.486 --> 00:08:42.255
that includes a couple of extra fields

175
00:08:42.255 --> 00:08:46.225
